[12.433401,"o","Assistant: That's just because utils is in the parent dir - but the original agent.ts has the same import path and works fine because of how the worker is loaded. Let me check the actual loading mechanism — it creates a Blob from the file. The import `./utils` is resolved relative to where the worker code originated. Looking at main.ts, since the file is read as a Blob and worker is created from blob URL, the `./utils` import won't work from a blob URL... but it works in the original. Let me re-read.\r\nRunning command:\r\n----\r\n\r\ncd /home/zabirauf/crumb && ls AGENTS/base-agent/\r\n\r\n----\r\n"]
[13.042716,"o","Assistant: There's no `utils.ts` symlink in the agent folder. But the original code imports `./utils`. This must be working somehow with Bun's worker resolution from blob URLs — perhaps Bun resolves it relative to cwd. Anyway, this is the same as the original so it should work. The error was just bun trying to bundle. Let me do a typecheck instead:\r\nRunning command:\r\n----\r\n\r\ncd /home/zabirauf/crumb && bunx --bun tsc --noEmit --target esnext --module esnext --moduleResolution bundler --allowImportingTsExtensions false --strict false AGENTS/base-agent/agent.ts 2>&1 | head -40\r\n\r\n----\r\n"]
[15.808417,"o","Assistant: These TS errors also exist in the original file (the original uses the same union type and the same loose access). Since the worker doesn't run a type-check it'll run fine at runtime. But let me fix the one new error I introduced — `clear_conversation` missing `toolResult`. Original had `{ action: \"clear_conversation\", toolResult: any | undefined }` and original returns just `{ action }`. So that's identical to original. All errors match the original code style.\r\n\r\nNow let me think about correctness:\r\n\r\n1. The `for (const ch of chunk)` iterates by code units, which is fine for ASCII chars.\r\n2. The escape sequence handling: when user presses arrow keys, we get `\\x1b[A` etc. - I early return on `0x1b` for the entire `chunk`. But that means subsequent characters in the same chunk get dropped. Fine for arrow keys.\r\n3. Issue: paste of multiline content — we early-return on `\\x1b`. Acceptable.\r\n4. The `getInput` for command permission prompts: after getInput returns, I call `render` which clears the screen. That works.\r\n"]
[15.808503,"o","\r\nLet me also think about Ctrl-D returning \"/exit\" — the agent could interpret that. Actually I should just exit the process there. Let me also consider — the `process.stdin` in a Worker may not be TTY at all. Workers in Bun share stdin? Let me check the fallback handling — `getInputFromUser` from utils uses `for await (const line of console)` which is what the original uses. That works in workers.\r\n\r\nThe TTY detection — in a Worker, `process.stdin.isTTY` might be undefined/false. Then we fall back to the readline path. But in the fallback I do `process.stdout.write(\"\\n\")` and try `for await`. That should still work because original code uses the same approach.\r\n\r\nHowever, there's a subtle issue: if not in TTY, we render the screen, write a newline, then wait for input — but the rendered screen has the cursor positioned in the input box and the input won't be visible. Let me improve the fallback. Actually, since Bun workers do have access to stdin via `console` async iterator, and `process.stdin.isTTY` may not be true in a worker context, the raw-mode path might never trigger. Let me test it.\r\nRunning command:\r\n----\r\n\r\ncd /home/zabirauf/crumb && cat > /tmp/tty_test.ts << 'EOF'\r\ndeclare var self: Worker;\r\nself.onmessage = () => {\r\n    const s: any = process.stdin;\r\n    postMessage({isTTY: s.isTTY, hasSetRawMode: typeof s.setRawMode === \"function\", platform: process.platform});\r\n};\r\nEOF\r\n\r\ncat > /tmp/tty_main.ts << 'EOF'\r\nconst file = Bun.file(\"/tmp/tty_test.ts\");\r\nconst blob = new Blob([await file.arrayBuffer()], {type: \"application/typescript\"});\r\nconst w = new Worker(URL.createObjectURL(blob));\r\nw.onmessage = (e) => { console.log(\"got:\", e.data); process.exit(0); };\r\nw.addEventListener(\"open\", () => w.postMessage({}));\r\nsetTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 3000);\r\nEOF\r\n\r\nbun run /tmp/tty_main.ts\r\n\r\n----\r\n"]
[16.67318,"o","Assistant: Worker has TTY access. The raw-mode path will be active. \r\n\r\nNow there's a concern: this base agent runs in a Bun worker. We can call `setRawMode` on it but main.ts also might be reading stdin? No, looking at main.ts, only the worker uses stdin (via utils.getInputFromUser). Good — no conflict.\r\n\r\nOne concern: when the Worker terminates and a new one starts on restart, the raw mode state needs cleaning. My code does `setRawMode(false)` on `cleanup()` after each input. Good.\r\n\r\nNow let me reconsider one issue: when the agent calls `setInterval` for the thinking indicator and overlaps with `callModel`, then we call `showThinking(false)` which renders the screen. But if we are not in the input loop, that's just a static render — no input expected at that moment. Then `handleResponse` may push messages and call `render` again. Fine.\r\n\r\nLet me also verify that I handle the resize event properly — `process.stdout.on` likely doesn't exist; should be `process.stdout.on?.()`. Already used optional chain. Good.\r\n\r\nLet me restart the base agent to apply the new runtime:\r\nAgent Restarting...\r\n"]
